# {{name}}

A WebAssembly component for Ouroboros lane W, in world `ouroboros:capability@0.1.0`.

## Build

```
rustup target add wasm32-wasip2
cargo build --release --target wasm32-wasip2
```

The artifact is `target/wasm32-wasip2/release/{{crate_name}}.wasm`. It is a **component**, not
a core module: `wasm32-wasip2` emits one directly.

## The loop

```
ouro wasm inspect target/wasm32-wasip2/release/{{crate_name}}.wasm
ouro wasm run     target/wasm32-wasip2/release/{{crate_name}}.wasm \
    --config '{}' --message '{"hello":"world"}'
```

`inspect` says what the component declares, how its shape sits against the bounds that decide
whether it compiles at all, and whether the node would admit it — as a capability, as a hook
component, or as neither, naming the refusal. `run` stands one instance up under the node's own
default bounds and sends it messages; every message goes to the same instance, because state in
this world is instance-held.

Both need the helper, and find it in exactly three places: `--helper <path>`, an absolute
`$OUROBOROS_WASM_HELPER`, or beside the `ouro` binary. Never the working directory — the helper
is the containment boundary, so a checkout must not be able to supply it.

## What this component may do

One import: `log`. No clock, no randomness, no filesystem, no socket. An import the host's
linker does not define fails to instantiate, which is the whole containment claim — so adding a
dependency that reaches for `std`'s stdio or environment does not produce a component with more
authority, it produces a component that will not run.

Three bounds, none of them optional and all of them per message: fuel, a wall-clock deadline,
and a memory ceiling summed across every memory the instance holds. A guest that exceeds one is
stopped and the host says which.

## Deploying it

Signing and rollout are the node's: see `docs/WASM.md` §7.5 and §7.6. A workspace hook needs no
signature — declare it in `ouroboros.toml`:

```toml
[[hooks]]
event = "PreToolUse"
component = "./hooks/{{crate_name}}.wasm"
matcher = "bash|write"
```

and check it with `ouro wasm check`, which judges the file the way a workspace nobody trusts
would be judged.
